{
  "abstract": "We study selections $S$ in the window $[0,2p)^2$, $p$ an odd prime, whose reduction modulo $p$ is a prescribed residue set $R$. Paper 2 proved $|S|\\le 3(p-1)$ when $R$ is an arc. We drop the arc condition in the four queen directions. Call $R$ of class q if every residue line of slope in $\\{2,\\dots,p-2\\}$ holds at most two residues, while residue rows, columns and $\\pm1$ diagonals are unrestricted. Theorem: if $S$ has at most two points on every row, column and $\\pm1$ diagonal of the window and $R$ is of class q, then $|S|\\le 3p+122=\\frac32 n+122$, where $n=2p$. The proof combines capacity counts with two variants of Segre's lemma of tangents in the coordinate-free form of Ball and Lavrauw. One variant is restricted to points on no line with three points of the set. The other has two special pencils (the diagonal directions), and there the lemma is applied to a multiset with multiplicity $-E$ at the two special points. A permutation case gives the sharper bound $|S|\\le|R|+2p$, and a splitting lemma charges residues on very rich diagonals at most 8 points per line. Computations check each new ingredient. The cyclic identity behind the two-pencil lemma holds on about 8000 triangles at $p=11,13,17$, and a perturbed negative control fails on every triangle. The permutation theorem holds exhaustively at $p=5,7$. Exact maxima show that the additive constant is not $-3$: genuinely no-three-in-line class-q witnesses have $16=3p+1$ points ($p=5$, optimal) and $20=3p-1$ points ($p=7$). Consequence: at side $2p$, a residue-position certificate can beat density $3/2$ by more than a constant only if its support has residue triples on generic lines. Light-safe supports may carry about $\\sqrt p$ residues per generic line. We measure, but do not prove, that the Hall-Jackson-Sudbery-Wild conic is locally isolated in that class.",
